:name: RP2350
:description: Raspberry Pi RP2350 with both Cortex-M33 cores and the boot ROM stand-in.

# Creates the machine and loads the boot ROM, nothing else: both cores then
# sit in the ROM, core 0 for want of an image and core 1 waiting to be
# launched. Include this from a script that goes on to load an image, the way
# rpi_pico2.resc does.

$name?="rp2350"
$platform?=$ORIGIN/../platforms/rp2350.repl

# The boot ROM stand-in is built with every image for the cortex_m33 board
# and left next to it as rp2350_bootrom.elf. That is where it is looked for,
# next to $elf, unless $bootrom says otherwise.
python "import os; monitor.Parse('$bootrom?=@' + os.path.join(os.path.dirname(str(monitor.GetVariable('elf'))), 'rp2350_bootrom.elf'))"

using sysbus
mach create $name

# The models that are not part of Renode. The order matters: a model can only
# refer to models that were compiled before it.
include $ORIGIN/../peripherals/RP2350_RegisterBlock.cs
include $ORIGIN/../peripherals/RP2350_AtomicAlias.cs
include $ORIGIN/../peripherals/RP2350_SysInfo.cs
include $ORIGIN/../peripherals/RP2350_BootRam.cs
include $ORIGIN/../peripherals/RP2350_Pads.cs
include $ORIGIN/../peripherals/RP2350_IOBank0.cs
include $ORIGIN/../peripherals/RP2350_SIO.cs
include $ORIGIN/../peripherals/RP2350_CortexM33.cs
include $ORIGIN/../peripherals/RP2350_PSM.cs
include $ORIGIN/../peripherals/RP2350_Resets.cs
include $ORIGIN/../peripherals/RP2350_Clocks.cs
include $ORIGIN/../peripherals/RP2350_Ticks.cs
include $ORIGIN/../peripherals/RP2350_Timer.cs
include $ORIGIN/../peripherals/RP2350_Watchdog.cs

machine LoadPlatformDescription $platform

showAnalyzer uart0

# Renode runs the macro called "reset" again after every reset of the machine.
macro reset
"""
    sysbus LoadELF $bootrom
"""
runMacro $reset
